{
  "dom_id": "step-q352199",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14224622/assets/1d8d3dfa1134b7fd5861ff70e493ead2227dd2d322e8436e28a903e0c596a408.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curves $x=4y-{y}^{2},$ $y=\\sqrt{x},$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above. Calculate the volume $V$ of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we write the second function in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ x & ={y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $f(y)=4y-{y}^{2}$ and $g(y)={y}^{2}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 4y-{y}^{2} & ={y}^{2} \\\\ 4y-2{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2y(2-y) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution corresponding to our region is $y=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14224622/assets/c5a117e2af85e3380942939812442eac7b899a2259af79ad032654fc5662a767.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)={y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=4y-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y([4y-{y}^{2}]-[{y}^{2}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(4y-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(4{y}^{2}-2{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi (4{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi ({4\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2}-{2\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (4(\\frac{8}{3}-0)-2(\\frac{16}{4}-0)) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{32}{3}-8) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
